# T5082 — DHCPv6 - EUI-64 and /64-/128 Advertised Prefix — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-30 — FAIL

Verdict: FAIL. The case expects both illegal configurations to be refused with an error ("Setting
illegal prefix should have error message and should not be accepted"). Terrence expected both
actions to be PREVENTED. The DUT prevented NEITHER of them (awplus_main-20260923-20, stack master
member 3):
- (1) PD prefix + `eui64` at a prefix length above /64: `ipv6 address <pd-name> ::/65|/96|/128 eui64`
  was ACCEPTED with no `% ` line, and all three appear in the running-config. The same check on
  a literal prefix IS enforced: `ipv6 address 2001:db8:5083::/65 eui64` →
  `% Prefix length must not be more than 64`, and `/128` → `% Bad mask /128 for address 2001:db8:5083::`.
  The PD path skips the check that the literal path applies.
- (2) Advertised prefix longer than /64: `ipv6 nd prefix 2001:db8:5082::/65`, `/96`, `/127` and `/128`
  were ACCEPTED with no `% ` line, as was `ipv6 nd prefix <pd-name> ::/65` and `::/128`. The DUT then
  ADVERTISED /65, /96, /127 and /128 in its Router Advertisements, each with L=1 and A=1 (autonomous
  address configuration on). That is six RAs captured on tb470 eth1.

Run labelled: clean. The one open item: no delegated prefix reached the DUT on this bench, so the
addresses from the accepted PD + eui64 lines could not be exercised (Observation O-1). The grade
does not depend on it.

Operator: bench-runner subagent (sentinel = parent session device-testing-ba, /test-mode shape A).
Queue: IE520/CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md row 14. Case text: ck.db zephyr AWPTCM-T5082, one step:
"Set client to get ipv6 address prefix from PD delegation server and eui-64. Advertised prefix should
be /64-/128. from CR00036691 … Try to advertise illegal prefix from CR00036689" → expected "(prefix
/64-/128 should not be allowed) ??? Setting illegal prefix should have error message and should not be
accepted." Terrence's decision (2026-09-30): "ignore the CR context, try to do the two things and see
if they let you or not. We expect both actions are prevented."

